      "deploying": false,
      "description": "This market will resolve to \"Yes\" if a deal in which Ukraine agrees to formally recognize Russian sovereignty over any UN-recognized Ukrainian territory is reached between the Russian Federation and Ukraine by December 31, 2025, 11:59 PM ET. Otherwise, this market will resolve to “No”.\n\nThe deal must be a publicly announced mutual agreement. \n\nIf such an agreement is officially reached before the resolution date, this market will resolve to \"Yes\", regardless of if/when the agreement goes into effect.\n\nAn agreement by Ukraine formally recognizing Russian sovereignty over any UN-recognized Ukrainian territory for any amount of time will count.\n\nAn agreement by Ukraine formally recognizing Russian sovereignty over any UN-recognized Ukrainian territory as a precondition of a more comprehensive peace process or deal will qualify, even if the agreement is not finalized or part of a formalized peace deal. The September 8, 1995, “Agreed Basic Principles” between Bosnia and Yugoslavia, which recognized the borders and sovereignty of Bosnia and Herzegovina, and was later formalized through the Dayton Peace Agreement, is an example of a qualifying agreement.\n\nAn official unilateral pledge by Ukraine formally recognizing Russian sovereignty over any UN-recognized Ukrainian territory will qualify for a “Yes” resolution even if not part of an agreement with Russia.\n\nThe April 19, 2013, Brussels Agreement between Serbia and Kosovo, in which Serbia recognized Kosovo’s de facto administration but did not formally grant de jure recognition, would not be considered a qualifying agreement under this market, as this market requires formal recognition of sovereignty rather than acknowledgment of administrative control.\n\nThe primary resolution source for this market will be an official announcement by Ukraine and/or the Russian Federation, however, an overwhelming consensus of credible reporting confirming an agreement has been reached will also qualify.\n",
      "enableNegRisk": false,
      "enableOrderBook": true,
      "endDate": "2027-01-01T04:59:00Z",
      "estimateValue": false,
      "eventMetadata": {
        "context_description": "Russia maintains that formal Ukrainian recognition of its control over Crimea and the four annexed eastern and southern regions remains a core precondition for any peace settlement, reiterated by its UN representative on September 1, 2026. Ukraine continues to reject this outright, with President Zelenskyy and senior officials stating that territorial integrity within 1991 borders is non-negotiable and that any such recognition would violate the constitution. Recent U.S. diplomatic activity, including planned envoy visits to both capitals announced September 3, has raised limited expectations for renewed talks, yet battlefield realities show Russia holding de facto control while Ukraine sustains strikes on Russian territory. Scheduled U.S.-mediated discussions and any potential referendums or constitutional changes in Kyiv represent the main near-term catalysts that could shift implied probabilities.",
