      "deploying": false,
      "description": "This market will resolve to \"Yes\" if any member state formally withdraws from NATO or provides an official notice of denunciation to NATO by December 31, 2025, 11:59 PM ET. Otherwise, this market will resolve to \"No\".\n\nA notice of denunciation refers to the submission of a notice of withdrawal as per Article 13 of the North Atlantic Treaty.\n\nA country's exit from NATO’s integrated military command structure will not be sufficient to resolve this market to \"Yes\". That country must either withdraw or submit a notice of denunciation to trigger a \"Yes\" resolution.\n\nThe resolution source will be official information from the relevant government and NATO, however a consensus of credible reporting may also be used.",
      "enableNegRisk": false,
      "enableOrderBook": true,
      "endDate": "2027-01-01T04:59:00Z",
      "estimateValue": false,
      "eventMetadata": {
        "context_description": "US President Donald Trump’s repeated April 2026 threats to withdraw the United States from NATO over European allies’ refusal to support operations in the Iran conflict drove early market attention, though he later stated at the July Ankara summit that the US would remain. Domestic US law requires congressional approval for any American exit, creating a significant procedural barrier. French presidential candidates Marine Le Pen and Jean-Luc Mélenchon have signaled intent to exit NATO’s integrated command or leave the alliance outright ahead of the 2027 vote, while European members have accelerated defense spending increases and contingency planning. No member has initiated Article 13 withdrawal procedures, and trader consensus reflects these legal, political, and diplomatic hurdles as the dominant constraints on near-term exits.",
        "context_requires_regen": false,
        "context_updated_at": "<timestamp>"
